\n- $(\\textrm{T}4)$ - Translation by $2$ units to the left\n\nWhat could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157198 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get\n\n$$\ny=-f(x-2)+3\n$$\n\nwe could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)$: Translate $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-2)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)$: Reflect $y=f(x-2)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2)$.\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)$: Translate $y=-f(x-2)$ by $3$ units up to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2)+3$.\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\qquad$ $(\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2) \\to (\\textrm{T}1)$." 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186157198 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-f(x-2)+3,$ we could apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}3)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=f(x)$ by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(x-2).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}2)\\mathbin{:}$ Reflect $y=f(x-2)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(x-2).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\textrm{T}1)\\mathbin{:}$ Translate $y=-f(x-2)$ by $3$ units up to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(x-2)+3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\qquad$ $(\\textrm{T}3) \\to (\\textrm{T}2) \\to (\\textrm{T}1).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157199 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x)$. Find the graph of $y=f\\left(-3x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/7a/7a0aac61b989b6aac26352c2f3faa05fc87ef37ec8ec9e120c83eabd213ed245.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186157199 1071 "The diagram below shows the function $y=f(x).$ Find the graph of $y=f\\left(-3x\\right).$\n\n![](images/7a/7a0aac61b989b6aac26352c2f3faa05fc87ef37ec8ec9e120c83eabd213ed245.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157199 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n2. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f$. We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the minus sign next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(3x\\right)$.\n\n![](images/c8/c858bf111c2dbc55aa3308276d5ee93aaa0f7a0b4e5b4b69e749f552bd548d57.png)\n\n- Then, reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-\\left(3x\\right)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/25/25bcd904d279c4e054e5d66b731ef294a3c1441d28e916c2824f99b9f16a3b7e.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186157199 1074 