lections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the negative sign next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo plot\n\n$$\ny=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)\n$$\n\nwe apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x)$.\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-x\\right)$ (remember that multiplication comes *before* subtraction in the order of operations).\n\n![](images/6c/6cde0cf55bd7a80cfb717eac89288dc99cede1b8456a0140d3e30c741958793d.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/80/803eb49b759eb31716470db93878fd2f3b73aaa0d8085bacd4be5a5f6706254d.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)$.\n\n![](images/37/3781afe1b28d36c73f2831461fee136d93a6bfe4439f47bb96dfb462a62677e2.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186157200 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the negative sign next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right)\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-x\\right)$ (remember that multiplication comes *before* subtraction in the order of operations).\n\n![](images/6c/6cde0cf55bd7a80cfb717eac89288dc99cede1b8456a0140d3e30c741958793d.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $1$ unit to the right to get the graph of $y=f\\left(-(x-1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/80/803eb49b759eb31716470db93878fd2f3b73aaa0d8085bacd4be5a5f6706254d.png)\n\n- Finally, stretch the result by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=3f\\left(-(x-1)\\right).$\n\n![](images/37/3781afe1b28d36c73f2831461fee136d93a6bfe4439f47bb96dfb462a62677e2.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157280 1038 17592186554566 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157280 1038 17592186554627 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157281 1038 17592186554618 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157291 1038 17592186554617 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157319 1038 17592186554539 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157319 1038 17592186554543 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157378 1038 17592186554548 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157378 1038 17592186554558 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157378 1038 17592186554611 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157379 1038 17592186554580 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157379 1038 17592186554643 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157380 1038 17592186554553 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157380 1038 17592186554557 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157380 1038 17592186554607 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157380 1038 17592186554624 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157483 1050 420.0 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157496 1050 780.0 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157629 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157629 1071 "The graph of $y =-f(2x) + 4$, can be obtained from the graph of $y = f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(T1)$ - Translation by $4$ units up\n- $(T2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n- $(T3)$ - Translation by $4$ units down\n- $(T4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186157629 1071 "The graph of $y=-f(2x)+4,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Translation by $4$ units up\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Translation by $4$ units down\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186157629 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-f(2x)+4,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}1):$ Translate $y=-f(2x)$ up by $4$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-f(2x)+4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2)\\to (\\text{T}1).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186158472 1064 "The **union** of two sets ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) denoted ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) is the set of all elements belonging to either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\nFor example, consider the sets ![](images/32/3228232fb4c16493d5268b308ede8727a84c20297d9b216a74db7b537011c704.svg) and ![](images/4d/4d576c7a3682975f73724c0fbddaf962449049968b852a28434102128ffa8a96.svg) We can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/8351db4817ac8b0d2991fe8ef09ee1f2583c84cb772114ef0614a0ef96c96389.png)\n\nThe union ![](images/58/58a13b2e88b95997772105b179139f2bb67105050519a451c17b838337cb4632.svg) consists of all the elements that are in ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. To represent ![](images/18/18271071563c5a2bcf483504921bb3fad4cbeedba4ca812bb20ef7ba96f637b7.svg) we will shade in all the area that is contained within either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both.\n\n![](images/e9/e912fe53d530ac47245c916bd16f1758df487ded32da88abfab253ed1ee0cc63.png)\n\nThe elements ![](images/0e/0e9479f799610ac47cadcc6890bb35f5336c08c9e2827384fd299138b7e4c558.svg) are in either ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) or ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) or both. Therefore, the union of ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/75/7533ad047439b7d799dd7afee5d6a7bc9f4404fbadb8c3f27ef6d3952119ff6b.svg) is\n\n![](images/dc/dc7e001d55359f56770a3cdd382a7318d77cbb12b260141399192d14953b4146.svg)\n\nNotice that ![](images/a7/a77a353782459b753313d80235712695d65dc4a44d8d357aa41add6729f81d8f.svg) belongs to both ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/65/65ada9c919fe5c4bee19fb4cafff28ed46a36bf6fc90b4cecf70e0fa02386ce0.svg) but we list it only once in ![](images/bb/bbb285be6a3e138649aa4a514c34823a6b39d094dca3427eb34eb4a326854fcf.svg) In a set, each distinct element is written only once." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186158472 1064 "The **union** of two sets $A$ and $B$, denoted $A \\cup B$, is the set of all elements belonging to either $A$ or $B$, or both.\n\nFor example, consider the sets\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,b,c \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{b, d, e \\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent these sets using a Venn diagram, as follows:\n\n![](images/83/8351db4817ac8b0d2991fe8ef09ee1f2583c84cb772114ef0614a0ef96c96389.png)\n\nThe union $A \\cup B$ consists of all the elements that are in $A$ or $B$, or both. To represent $A \\cup B$, we will shade in all the area that is contained within either $A$ or $B$, or both.\n\n![](images/e9/e912fe53d530ac47245c916bd16f1758df487ded32da88abfab253ed1ee0cc63.png)\n\nThe elements $a,b,c,d,e$ are in either $A$ or $B$, or both. Therefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA \\cup B = \\{a,b,c,d,e \\}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186158478 1064 "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize unions, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a union.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets\n\n$$\nA = \\{a,b,c \\}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nB = \\{b, d, e \\}\n$$\n\nThis time, we will compute the union $A \\cup B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B$, or both.\n\nSo, we will start with the elements of $A$, and then go through the elements of $B$ to look for any elements that we do not already have. Remember that we are looking for elements that we do *not* already have in $A$.\n\n- For the element $b \\in B$, we have $b \\in A. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n- For the element $d \\in B$, we have $d \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n- For the element $e \\in B$, we have $e \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nSo, to get the union, we need to take the elements of $A$ and also include $d$ and $e$.\n\nTherefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA \\cup B = \\{a,b,c,d,e \\}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186158478 1064 "Although Venn diagrams can be useful to visualize unions, we do not have to draw a Venn diagram every time we wish to compute a union.\n\nFor example, let's again consider the sets $A = \\{ a,b,c \\}$ and $B = \\{ b, d, e \\}.$ This time, we will compute the union $A \\cup B$ without using a Venn diagram. We just need to find all the elements that are in either $A$ or $B,$ or both.\n\nSo, we will start with the elements of $A,$ and then go through the elements of $B$ to look for any elements that we do not already have. Remember that we are looking for elements that we do *not* already have in $A.$\n\n- For the element $b \\in B,$ we have $b \\in A. \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- For the element $d \\in B,$ we have $d \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\n- For the element $e \\in B,$ we have $e \\not \\in A. \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nSo, to get the union, we need to take the elements of $A$ and also include $d$ and $e.$\n\nTherefore, the union of $A$ and $B$ is\n\n$$\nA \\cup B = \\{ a,b,c,d,e \\}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186158543 1064 "Given the graph of a curve $y= f(x)$, there are two types of reflections we can apply to it:\n\n1. Reflection across the $y$-axis. This is a transformation of the form $y=f(-x)$.\n2. Reflection across the $x$-axis. This is a transformation of the form $y=-f(x)$.\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ below.\n\n![](images/69/6931216dd63985631394e3f3f2fe59246ddd810ecce5af0f0c7ad18b8634133b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(-x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e2/e2274a68c25ed14ba93410fe87d672f85b839b7214b30e80d34a3ae28f47ef03.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ee/eee560bdd17899d55cdaea35204e9963ae9f0148f0ef897173e6250a3eb874b3.png)\n\nWe can also combine reflections with other graph transformations. Let's see how." 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186158543 1064 "Given the graph of a curve $y= f(x),$ there are two types of reflections we can apply to it:\n\n1\\. Reflection across the $y$-axis. This is a transformation of the form \n\n  $$\n  y=f(-x).\n  $$\n\n2\\. Reflection across the $x$-axis. This is a transformation of the form \n\n  $$\n  y=-f(x).\n  $$\n\nFor example, let's consider the function $y=f(x)$ below. \n\n![](images/69/6931216dd63985631394e3f3f2fe59246ddd810ecce5af0f0c7ad18b8634133b.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(-x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $y$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/e2/e2274a68c25ed14ba93410fe87d672f85b839b7214b30e80d34a3ae28f47ef03.png)\n\n \n\nThe graph of $y=-f(x)$ is a reflection of $y=f(x)$ in the $x$-axis, and it looks as follows:\n\n![](images/ee/eee560bdd17899d55cdaea35204e9963ae9f0148f0ef897173e6250a3eb874b3.png)\n\n  \n\nWe can also combine reflections with other graph transformations. Let's see how." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1075 17592186165034 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186165033 1075 17592186165035 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186165033 1075 17592186165036 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186165033 1075 17592186165037 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186165033 1075 17592186165038 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186165033 1075 17592186553901 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1075 17592186553932 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1075 17592186554078 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1075 17592186554088 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1075 17592186554420 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186165033 1078 17592186554420 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186166962 1038 17592186554574 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186166968 1038 17592186554590 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186166968 1038 17592186554632 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186166969 1038 17592186554626 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186166969 1038 17592186554636 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186167304 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186167304 1071 "Let $B = {2, 4, 6, 8}$. Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5 ∈ B]$?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186167304 1071 "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[5\\in B]?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186167304 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[5\\in B]$ has $x=5$ and $A=B.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[5\\in B]={1}_{B}(5).\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1075 17592186168886 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186168885 1075 17592186168887 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186168885 1075 17592186168888 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186168885 1075 17592186168889 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186168885 1075 17592186168890 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186168885 1075 17592186554033 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1075 17592186554092 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1075 17592186554107 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1075 17592186554113 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1075 17592186554134 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186168885 1078 17592186554092 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1075 17592186169096 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186169095 1075 17592186169097 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186169095 1075 17592186169098 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186169095 1075 17592186169099 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186169095 1075 17592186169100 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186169095 1075 17592186553922 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1075 17592186553941 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1075 17592186554164 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1075 17592186554261 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1075 17592186554406 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186169095 1078 17592186554164 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186171688 1071 "Let $\\widehat{\\,y}=\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_0+\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1x=1.6+0.7  x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $(x_1,y_1), \\: \\ldots, \\: (x_{8},y_{8})$ of $n=8$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.25,$ and $S_{xx} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})^2 = 42,$ find a $90\\%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T > 1.943) = 0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $6$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for $\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{SE}[\\widehat{\\,\\beta}_1\\,] = \\sqrt{ \\dfrac{\\textrm{MSE}}{S_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186171688 1071 "Let