ing:\n\n- Each data point in the sample has some *residual* (i.e., error) associated with it. The error of our regression model at the data point $(x_i,y_i)$ is given by \n\n$$\n(y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i).\n$$\n\n- The expression $\\displaystyle\\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i - \\widehat{\\,y}_i)^2$ gives the sum of the squared residuals of our regression model.\n\n- Even considering the entire population, the data points will not fall on a perfectly straight line. Thus, errors are present even in the \"true\" regression model. These errors have some (unknown) variance, which we'll denote as $\\sigma^2.$\n\n- It can be shown that the $\\textrm{MSE}$ is an *unbiased* estimator of $\\sigma^2.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186173249 1064 "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1,\n\\qquad\n\\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i.\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})\\overset{―}{y} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}n\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\overset{―}{y}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}){y}_{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy},$ we can write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{{S}_{xx}}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{ x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}.\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{E}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}\\text{E}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}({\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}+{\\beta }_{1}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{x}_{i} \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{0}\\cdot 0+{\\beta }_{1}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & ={\\beta }_{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n\n- For the variance, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[\\overset{^}{{\\beta }_{1}}] & =\\text{Var}[{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\text{Var}[{y}_{i}] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2}\\cdot {\\sigma }^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2}\\cdot {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{k}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{{S}_{xx}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right).\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186173249 1064 "Earlier, we made use of the following results:\n\n$$\n\\textrm E[\\widehat{\\beta}_1] = \\beta_1, \\qquad \\textrm{Var}[\\widehat{\\beta_1}] = \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nLet's now derive these. First, we will show that\n\n$$\nS_{xy} = \\sum_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})y_i\n$$\n\nWe can show this is true using some straightforward manipulations of the summation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS_{xy} &= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))(y_{i} - y^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―)) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)nx^(―) \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i} - y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} + y^(―)∑_(i = 1)^(n)x_{i} \\\\\n&= ∑_(i = 1)^(n)(x_{i} - x^(―))y_{i}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing our result for $S_{xy}$, we can write\n\n$$\nβ_{1}^(^) = \\frac{S_{xy}}{S_{xx}} = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\nk_i = \\dfrac{x_i-\\overline{x}}{S_{xx}}\n$$\n\nThe following properties can be shown using straightforward algebra:\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i=0$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i x_i=1$\n- $\\displaystyle \\sum_{i=1}^n k_i^2=\\dfrac{1}{S_{xx}}$\n\nNow, we compute the mean and variance of $\\widehat{\\beta_1}$ as follows:\n\n- For the mean, we have\n$E[β_{1}^(^)]|= E[∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}E[y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}(β_{0} + β_{1}x_{i}); = β_{0}∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i} + β_{1}∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{1}x_{i}; = β_{0} \\cdot 0 + β_{1} \\cdot 1; = β_{1}$.\nNote that we used the properties of $k_i$ discussed previously.\n- For the variance, we have\n$Var[β_{1}^(^)]|= Var[∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2}Var[y_{i}]; = ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2} \\cdot σ^{2}; = σ^{2} \\cdot ∑_(i = 1)^(n)k_{i}^{2}; = \\frac{σ^{2}}{S_{xx}}$.\n\nSo, finally, we have\n\n$$\n\\widehat{\\beta_1} \\sim N\\left(\\beta_1, \\dfrac{\\sigma^2}{S_{xx}}\\right)\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186174298 1075 17592186174299 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186174298 1075 17592186174300 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186174298 1075 17592186174301 13194139535995 17592186045417 false] [17592186174298 1075 17592186174303 13194139535995 17592186045417 false]