 [17592186554536 1077 "selection" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554536 1078 17592186554052 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554536 1079 #uuid "fcab1227-d753-5449-9bf0-127c2147993a" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1075 17592186554105 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1075 17592186554169 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1075 17592186554209 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1075 17592186554269 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1075 17592186554390 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1076 17592186045493 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1077 "selection" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1078 17592186554169 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554537 1079 #uuid "fcc2d5ce-3092-541f-9a44-9ec3c17048dd" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1075 17592186553966 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1075 17592186554041 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1075 17592186554152 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1075 17592186554303 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1075 17592186554357 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1076 17592186045493 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1077 "selection" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1078 17592186553966 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554538 1079 #uuid "ff946275-1749-5fd4-bd3f-74106924808a" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1068 17592186554537 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1069 "q-98016" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1071 "![](images/0b/0b2dd71389af5f66bc7931819038922728c3533f89d0de9234514ee24a9d33a5.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)-3?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1072 #uuid "044825ff-2a34-548f-8e31-b6e40bc76ccd" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554539 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)-3,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](images/e2/e2b35429545d9e5c8b4e9217908b4c3755790425ff87cfb699da7aae47f6dc6b.png)\n\n- Then, translate the result down by $3$ units to get the graph of $y=f(-x)-3.$\n\n![](images/1b/1b81c0a3c72fa80d35d5dccb462afeb2ba3f1b05c10f009f300fdfd170ed9e76.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1068 17592186554452 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1069 "q-3047" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{45{y}^{2}}$ given that $y\\ge 0.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1072 #uuid "09d71431-cc0e-5153-8350-c0a86eafb77b" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554540 1074 "We first write $45{y}^{2}$ as the product $9{y}^{2}\\cdot 5$ which contains a perfect square $(9{y}^{2}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{45{y}^{2}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{9{y}^{2}\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}\\cdot 5} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(3y)^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ |3y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ |3|\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $y\\ge 0,$ we have $|y|=y,$ and we can simplify further. Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\cdot |y|\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3\\cdot y\\cdot \\sqrt{5} & = \\\\ 3y\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1068 17592186554475 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1069 "q-102712" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1071 "Consider the surface ${z}^{2}y+16=4\\sqrt{x}+y.$ Find the equation of the tangent plane to that surface at the point $(4,-1,3).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1072 #uuid "138bcf11-9efc-57ee-ad7b-ef21818db0e7" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554541 1074 "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}y+16 & =4\\sqrt{x}+y. \\\\ {z}^{2}y-4\\sqrt{x}-y & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y,z)={z}^{2}y-4\\sqrt{x}-y$, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=-16.$ \n\nRecall that the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(4,-1,3):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({z}^{2}y-4\\sqrt{x}-y)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({z}^{2}y-4\\sqrt{x}-y)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({z}^{2}y-4\\sqrt{x}-y)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}\\text{ }i+({z}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+2zy\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇\\,f(4,-1,3) & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{4}}\\text{ }i+({3}^{2}-1)\\text{ }j+2(3)(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =-\\,i+8\\text{ }j-6\\,k \\\\  & =⟨-1,8,-6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(4,-1,3)\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y+1,\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨-1,8,-6⟩\\cdot ⟨x-4,\\,y+1,\\,z-3⟩ & =0 \\\\ -(x-4)+8(y+1)-6(z-3) & =0 \\\\ -x+8y-6z+30 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1068 17592186554486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1069 "q-159581" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1071 "| **Number of hours ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$3$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Grade ($y$)** | $75$ | $82$ | $90$ | $95$ |\n\nThe table above shows the number of hours four students spent studying for a math exam and the grades they obtained. The corresponding fitted linear regression line using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.9,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1072 #uuid "14b7fe25-0e56-53c7-82f0-cba7c7a1a3e7" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554542 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=61.7+6.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=61.7+6.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=61.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-3.5)^{2}+(3-3.5)^{2}+(4-3.5)^{2}+(5-3.5)^{2} \\\\  & =2.25+0.25+0.25+2.25 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{0.9\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{5})}≈10.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=61.7\\pm 10.9\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1068 17592186554449 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1069 "q-98012" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1071 "![](images/5d/5dca91f7173c10e2f274d8611e1308445c4acc72f48595880045143a68d532b1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=f(-x)+2?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1072 #uuid "1714eddf-429e-5900-b54a-c409f35eac75" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554543 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=f(-x)+2,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Reflect it across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-x).$\n\n![](images/1b/1b60eeb882c85423bb0550c2b6def0d073d53747479bf322976db313ffdf4109.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result  up by $2$ units to get the graph of $y=f(-x)+2.$\n\n![](images/58/58a681bf9aa47521b30761e904dad8e816e2077387c28b132d2480bb39ea4baa.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1068 17592186554532 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1069 "q-73907" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}+3{x}^{2}-x-3$, which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1072 #uuid "17f76fde-fb9f-52ba-9f93-d56954798f7f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554544 1074 "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & 1 & 3 & -1 & -3 \\\\  &  & -1 & -2 & 3 \\\\  & 1 & 2 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)({x}^{2}+2x-3) \\\\  & =(x+1)(x-1)(x+3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,$ $x=1,$ and $x=-3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+1)(0-1)(0+3) \\\\  & =(1)(-1)(3) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,-1,1$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/40/40b0fc54d72820b2893dba5160b4e5f1c70bf7b63aebd63eb3d5ea0ad078dcef.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1068 17592186554483 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1069 "q-42694" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1071 "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x-9y+4z=1 \\\\ 3x-2y-5z=7 \\\\ 3x-8y+2z=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{z}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1072 #uuid "189e5e9d-c4bc-5bb9-8c63-9649351614d1" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554545 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+(-9)y+4z=1 \\\\ 3x+(-2)y+(-5)z=7 \\\\ 3x+(-8)y+2z=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 & 4 \\\\ 3 & -2 & -5 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 2\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & -9 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -2 & 7 \\\\ 3 & -8 & 5\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1068 17592186554478 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1069 "q-28501" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=-\\frac{1}{6}(x+3)(x-2)(x-3)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1072 #uuid "1bca3374-bd66-579c-b0ab-3963f1fce03c" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554546 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{1}{6}(x+3)(x-2)(x-3)=-\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-3,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{6}(0+3)(0-2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-\\frac{(3)(-2)(-3)}{6} \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-3,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/28/2852cbbe5f7e3e9dfb02968b92d2d3f1eca405b972dc716167375979958645e4.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1068 17592186554435 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1069 "q-134824" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1071 "![](images/ae/ae6bc049d3bf3bbdf1a609b6cda4c3b4e9cc1699b387099d265e048a32be68d6.png)\n\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on a set of bivariate numerical data $(x,y).$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $x=2,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1072 #uuid "1f3c0f66-15fe-5ce9-aae2-49226c3a0199" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554547 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $x=2$ is positive. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}>\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model underestimates (*not* overestimates) this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as $x$ increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/2f/2f5f3a4dc75324a6ef415c987a62139e2dbd51c5b35a56d5a35f410781087c43.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1068 17592186554460 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1069 "q-97793" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1071 "The graph of $y=-3f(4x),$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1072 #uuid "1f3c3035-69e9-5d03-a0fc-4433cacd4260" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554548 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=-3f(4x),$ we could apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{4}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(4x)$ by a scale factor of $3$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=3f(4x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Reflect $y=3f(4x)$ across the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=-3f(4x).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1068 17592186554473 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1069 "q-134825" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1071 "![](images/57/57ba7828008f3e3c271ae4c1b6d444005f40c09bf6ba99e614f3b6414c1e56c2.png)\n\nAn employee of a car dealership collects data on the value $y$ (in thousands of dollars) of a particular car model $t$ years after its release to the market. The residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on this data set. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $t=6,$ the regression equation overestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1072 #uuid "21d8d0de-cc80-527b-ade5-cff77e1f75ae" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554549 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $t=6$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates this data point.\n\n- Statement II is false. The plot shows the residuals tend to decrease as time increases. So, there is a trend, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe18ccc94d0cc98f971b6482d4e31c7ec7066b5cec427108ddd8d1e6609e08ba.png)\n\n  \n\n- Statement III is false. Since the residuals are *not* randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, our regression line is *not* a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1068 17592186554512 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1069 "q-159566" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=3.5,$ the mean square error of the model is $2.1,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=85,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.921)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1072 #uuid "24ed500c-c9f9-51ed-ab79-e8e54e985d91" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554550 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.6-3.9x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.6-3.9x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=12.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.921)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.005}=2.921.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.921\\cdot \\sqrt{2.1\\cdot (\\frac{1}{18}+\\frac{{3.5}^{2}}{85})}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=12.6\\pm 1.9\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1068 17592186554446 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1068 17592186554509 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1069 "q-175915" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1071 "The equation of the line that passes through the points $(2,8)$ and $(4,4)$ is $\\,y=${{field-1}}\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1072 #uuid "2546e8f7-eee4-51d1-b227-0107b1dc1dff" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554551 1074 "We will write the equation of the line in the slope-intercept form $y=mx+b.$ So, we need to work out the slope $m$ and the $y$-intercept $b.$\n\nFirst, we calculate the slope $m$ using the given points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4-8}{4-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the slope $m=-2$ into the slope-intercept formula $y=mx+b,$ we reach\n\n$$\ny=-2x+b.\n$$\n\nWe now need to find the $y$-intercept $b.$ We can find this by substituting the coordinates of a point on the line. Any point will do, so let's substitute $(4,4):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-2x+b \\\\ 4 & =-2(4)+b \\\\ 4 & =-8+b \\\\ 4+8 & =b \\\\ 12 & =b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the equation of the line is $y=-2\\,x+12.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1068 17592186554484 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1069 "q-170399" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1071 "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=2{x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $3.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1072 #uuid "2573d8ba-3c24-5f3f-9b87-70aaa707f60f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554552 1074 "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=3:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-3I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-3[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-3I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1068 17592186554491 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1069 "q-17782" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1071 "![](images/ef/efc577f386353b4120dec733e5e6a627ad2412744dce4d71deba2b500a1edec2.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}x-1)?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1072 #uuid "269fb630-a40e-5592-b4e3-251a076c8d27" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554553 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)),$ we apply the following steps: \n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=2$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}x).$\n\n![](images/28/2891d009bd6f5d026ca47afe90f3ec4d6fafc654b7212392b325f76fe43f806e.png)\n\n \n\n- Then, translate the result by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/ab/ab1f210741e01139618dc2fa1347cf1591cb90ca4f67906b89ebc7df29d77f32.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{1}{2}(x-2)).$\n\n![](images/e5/e5552a14dc62407e02bb8f5e633ae194a95d313bce04abb3186b56ce83ec2974.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1068 17592186554436 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1069 "q-28733" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1071 "${(-{x}^{-3}y)}^{-5}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1072 #uuid "26bd12ec-8ad5-573b-9a35-f2693b66e2bb" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554554 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(-{x}^{-3}y)}^{-5} & = \\\\ {(-{x}^{-3})}^{-5}{(y)}^{-5} & = \\\\ (-x)^{(-3)(-5)}\\cdot {y}^{-5} & = \\\\ (-x)^{15}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{y}^{5}} & = \\\\ -\\frac{{x}^{15}}{{y}^{5}} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1068 17592186554519 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1069 "q-22657" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1071 "![](images/6d/6d6009de9ef5df47144f36eb42393b7e9f77ab5c70e3606db09e48bf98289ecb.png)\n\nWhich of the following polygons has the same perimeter as the hexagon $ABCDEF$ shown above?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1072 #uuid "29ae9066-cb5e-58ca-bc97-42812b49b53a" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554555 1074 "The perimeter of the triangle shown below is $17$, as well as the perimeter of the given hexagon. Among the options given, the triangle is the only one that satisfies this. \n\n![](images/e4/e40d126b8304f762f95026016e13c193994fbb7f70f3173a86a35a08526a3bdb.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1068 17592186554531 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1069 "q-102805" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1071 "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{x}$ is a solution to the equation $x{y}^{″}-(x+1){y}^{′}+y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1072 #uuid "2abfd6a6-feff-503c-bea4-5ba5b90fc5a2" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554556 1074 "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {e}^{x}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){e}^{x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x({u}^{″}{e}^{x}+2{u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})-(x+1)({u}^{′}{e}^{x}+u{e}^{x})+u{e}^{x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}x+ux)-{e}^{x}{u}^{′}(x+1)-{e}^{x}u(x+1)+{e}^{x}u & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+2{u}^{′}x+ux-{u}^{′}(x+1)-u(x+1)+u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(2x-x-1)+u(x-x-1+1)) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(x-1)) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{x}\\ne 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+{u}^{′}(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+w(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x{e}^{-x}\\,⟹\\,u=-{c}_{1}(1+x){e}^{-x}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{e}^{x}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}(-{c}_{1}(1+x){e}^{-x}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=(1+x).\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1068 17592186554497 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1069 "q-115018" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1071 "![](images/5b/5b3e36d9b39fcc8d61fe30219d2f3191a1b305a44995b27d5daddb5f18926e02.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1072 #uuid "2b3b851a-20c6-5158-b86d-a090b5acc8fc" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554557 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(x-2)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Translate it by $2$ units to the right to get the graph of $y=f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/48/482f1dc55995a299b21894c0ec191c0f8fb8213038cdc5f80da1e77a0d64af4e.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2).$\n\n![](images/a2/a2832ebdf82e2a7bfddccf24fd598758e1ce86fc0726f9c128503574b09fcd0b.png)\n\n- Finally, translate it down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-f(x-2)-4.$\n\n![](images/94/9473b7ebfbe7db3d1cfefcf3f7b616ffec4b9ad2e685a6fc40f4560e8d2a1c59.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1068 17592186554434 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1069 "q-97785" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1071 "The graph of $y=f(-2x)-1,$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $1$ unit down\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Stretch of scale factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1072 #uuid "2b4b8cea-e134-5e94-b33a-7dc117bff583" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554558 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-2x)-1,$ we could apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Stretch $y=f(x)$ by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}3):$ Reflect $y=f(2x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(2(-x))=f(-2x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-2x)$ down by $1$ unit to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-2x)-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}3)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1068 17592186554489 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1069 "q-3925" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1071 "${(16{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1072 #uuid "2ccfb4b8-1710-553d-964a-06ac5c1fded5" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554559 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(16{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {({4}^{2}{j}^{2}{k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {({4}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot {({j}^{2})}^{1/2}\\cdot {({k}^{6})}^{1/2} & = \\\\ {4}^{2\\cdot (1/2)}\\cdot {j}^{2\\cdot (1/2)}\\cdot {k}^{6\\cdot (1/2)} & = \\\\ {4}^{2/2}\\cdot {j}^{2/2}\\cdot {k}^{6/2} & = \\\\ 4j{k}^{3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1068 17592186554526 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1069 "q-162327" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1071 "![](images/d4/d4573a2e3c7b220ac363c996c5332a3db93cc48b5b941e46a529b6912804f0c6.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1-{y}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $y,$ and then $z.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1072 #uuid "31b80dba-d43f-591d-b1ec-e6cb3d1597c2" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554560 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{y}^{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 2-y-z\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/8d/8d1db6e886f00596814900ca0c4113590a37a407b65c800eb1f9926328ade50c.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 1-{y}^{2}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the right boundary of the region, written in the form $y=y(z),$ is \n\n$$\nz=1-{y}^{2}\\,⟹\\,y=\\sqrt{1-z}.\n$$\n\nand the left boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=0.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le \\sqrt{1-z}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/1f/1fe8a810a7e98c8a40739d5019ff9054a5375c0a12ae7fd1b17f8b6c8bb19f75.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{\\sqrt{1-z}}{\\int }_{0}^{2-y-z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1068 17592186554535 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1069 "q-95340" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $A$ given above. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${A}^{T}=A$\n\nII. ${A}^{2}=A$\n\nIII. $A$ is an orthogonal projection matrix" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1072 #uuid "3561f664-6b98-59a1-983b-c026ed37b09d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554561 1074 "Recall that $Q$ is an orthogonal projection matrix if and only if ${Q}^{2}=Q$ and ${Q}^{T}=Q.$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, ${A}^{T}=A.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Indeed, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{A}^{2} & =A\\cdot A \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{3} & \\frac{2}{3} \\\\ \\frac{2}{3} & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[\\begin{aligned}20 & 10 \\\\ 10 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{20}{9} & \\frac{10}{9} \\\\ \\frac{10}{9} & \\frac{5}{9}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & \\ne A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since not both statements I and II are true.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1068 17592186554506 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1069 "q-67512" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1071 "Find the equation for the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose $4$th term is $27$ and whose $11$th term is $-\\frac{1}{81}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1072 #uuid "3805f218-eb22-5e05-bae4-085089844c85" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554562 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=11$ and $n=4,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{4-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{3}, \\\\ {a}_{11} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{11-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{11}=-\\frac{1}{81},$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{3}=27 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{10}=-\\frac{1}{81}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{10}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{10}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{3}} & =\\frac{(-\\frac{1}{81})}{27} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{10}}{{r}^{3}} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187} \\\\ {r}^{7} & =-\\frac{1}{2\\,187}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1068 17592186554524 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1069 "q-75094" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1071 "Given that\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]$\nhas the LU decomposition $A=LU,$ then ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1072 #uuid "39a7d310-9046-5578-9f8d-31308eeefed9" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554563 1074 "To find the matrices $L$ and $U,$ we reduce the matrix $A$ to row echelon form. Whilst doing this, we simultaneously write down the row operations applied to $A$ in terms of matrix multiplication using elementary matrices.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -10 & -5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+4{R}_{1} & ⟺ & \\,\\overset{{E}_{1}}{\\overset{⏞}{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}}\\cdot A \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =U\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe reduced matrix is upper triangular, so it is our matrix $U.$ Now, since ${E}_{1}A=U,$ we can invert ${E}_{1}$ to arrive at the desired LU factorization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{E}_{1}A & =U \\\\ A & ={E}_{1}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1}U \\\\ A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{L}{{[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{U}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${l}_{21}=-4,{u}_{23}=3,$ and ${l}_{21}\\cdot {u}_{23}=-12.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1068 17592186554502 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1069 "q-33453" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1071 "Find the shortest distance between the point $P(-2,-8,-1)$ and the plane $Π$ with equation $2x+y-2z=-7.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1072 #uuid "39c46748-4786-53ad-a218-6f5fdd2db8c5" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554564 1074 "Let's start by picking a point on the plane. To do that, we simply substitute some concrete values for $x$ and $y$ into the equation and solve for $z.$ Let's choose $x=1$ and $y=-3$ (though it doesn't really matter what values we choose). Solving for $z,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+y-2z & =-7 \\\\ 2(1)+(-3)+7 & =2z \\\\ 6 & =2z \\\\ z & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get the point $Q(1,-3,3)$ which lies on the plane.\n\nComputing $\\overset{\\to }{PQ},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{PQ} & =⟨1,-3,3⟩-⟨-2,-8,-1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,5,4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe also need a normal vector to the plane. For any Cartesian equation of a plane $ax+by+cz=d,$ the normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩.$ So, for the plane $Π,$ we have $n=⟨2,1,-2⟩.$ \n\nFinally, the shortest distance from the point $P$ to the plane $Π$ is the absolute value of the scalar projection of $\\overset{\\to }{PQ}$ onto the normal vector of the plane:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =|{\\text{comp}}_{n}\\,\\overset{\\to }{PQ}| \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\overset{\\to }{PQ}\\cdot n|}{|n|} \\\\  & =\\frac{|3\\cdot 2+5\\cdot 1+4\\cdot (-2)|}{\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|6+5-8|}{\\sqrt{4+1+4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{|\\,3\\,|}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1068 17592186554444 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1069 "q-43120" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1071 "Consider the system of equations \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x-8y+5z=8 \\\\ -4x-6y+3z=-1 \\\\ x-4z=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $z=\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{z}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1072 #uuid "3cd38166-ab43-501c-8a09-bb660de655be" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554565 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(-2)x+(-8)y+5z=8 \\\\ (-4)x+(-6)y+3z=-1 \\\\ 1x+0y+(-4)z=-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 & 5 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}-2 & -8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -6 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -7\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1068 17592186554463 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1069 "q-102074" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1071 "If $X=\\{105,118,119,123,135\\}$ and $Y=\\{108,119,122,123\\}$, then $X\\cup Y=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1072 #uuid "3ece29a0-cc0f-5be6-9001-b8cc96566290" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554566 1074 "The set $X\\cup Y$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $X$ or $Y,$ or both. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X\\cup Y & =\\{105,118,119,123,135\\}\\cup \\{108,119,122,123\\} \\\\  & =\\{105,108,118,119,122,123,135\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1068 17592186554462 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1069 "q-141408" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1072 #uuid "41a5c3bd-58e0-5924-89f4-127ab078d31e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554567 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=2{(\\frac{2}{3})}^{n}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{2}{3}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1068 17592186554459 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1069 "q-53199" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1071 "![](images/06/06f965498f6415c08b32c5ae30235a8eec65d6dd67cd1f349e80bcbe88bd5495.png)\n\nA boy $B$ is flying a kite $K$ with a $20\\,\\text{m}$ length of string. If the kite is rising vertically at a constant speed of $1\\,\\text{m/s},$ at what rate is the angle of elevation $\\theta $ increasing when $\\theta ={45}^{∘}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1072 #uuid "4220ee9a-71ec-56c9-a4ec-d0480b90a44d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554568 1074 "![](images/23/236144ff93bd22f5c4460c31e20216c45d6f8ac85dc945321dadecc093f379e0.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the kite's height, and $z$ is the distance between the boy and the kite. The quantity $z=20$ meters does not change with time because the string's length doesn't change.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$\nTo do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{20} \\\\ y & =20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(20\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConverting the given information from degrees to radians, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=1$ and $\\theta ={45}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{{180}^{∘}}=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =(20\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{1}{20} & =(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle of elevation is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{20}$ radians per second." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1068 17592186554466 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1069 "q-159367" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $11.5,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=143,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.812)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1072 #uuid "42368253-8090-5197-973b-1010d704c08f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554569 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4+1.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4+1.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=1.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.812)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.05}=1.812.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.812\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{11.5}{143}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.8\\pm 0.5\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1068 17592186554505 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1069 "q-43121" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1071 "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+z=1 \\\\ 6x-2y-z=-14 \\\\ 3x+y-z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $27,$ then $z=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1072 #uuid "42ee0be6-4308-53bc-b367-0e542cac978e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554570 1074 "First, let's rewrite the system to reveal the coefficients:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x+3y+1z=1 \\\\ 6x+(-2)y+(-1)z=-14 \\\\ 3x+1y+(-1)z=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by writing the determinant of the coefficients matrix, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}|=27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find ${D}_{z}$, we replace the third column by the right-hand side of the system, and calculate the determinant:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{z}=|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -2 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & =2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & -14 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|-3\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -14 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}|+1\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}6 & -2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(-2+14)-3(6+42)+(6+6) \\\\  & =24-144+12 \\\\  & =-108.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying Cramer's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{{D}_{z}}{D}=\\frac{-108}{27}=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554571 1068 17592186554533 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554571 1069 "q-159393" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554571 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554571 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1-2.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{11},{y}_{11})$ of $n=11$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $0.78