e model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=0.6+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $80%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.8=0.2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.1.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.886)=0.1.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.1}=1.886.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(34-38)^{2}+(36-38)^{2}+(40-38)^{2}+(42-38)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=1.886\\cdot \\sqrt{0.0125\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{38}^{2}}{40})}≈1.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=0.6\\pm 1.3\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1068 17592186554518 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1069 "q-95363" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1071 "$$\na=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,P=\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}{p}_{11} & {p}_{12} & {p}_{13} \\\\ {p}_{21} & {p}_{22} & {p}_{23} \\\\ {p}_{31} & {p}_{32} & {p}_{33}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nGiven that the vector $a$ above spans ${S}^{⊥},$ and that $P$ is the orthogonal projection matrix of ${R}^{3}$ onto the subspace $S$, what is the value of ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1072 #uuid "555dd099-33d6-5089-b5ce-f831f111dabe" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554573 1074 "First, we find the orthogonal projection matrix onto ${S}^{⊥}=\\text{Span}\\{a\\}.$\n\nUsing the formula for the orthogonal projection matrix\n\n$$\n{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=a({a}^{T}\\,a)^{-1}{a}^{T},\n$$\n\nwe obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{([\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}])}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & ={6}^{-1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that ${P}_{S}+{P}_{{S}^{⊥}}=I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S} & =I-{P}_{{S}^{⊥}} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]-\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, ${p}_{12}+{p}_{33}=1+2=3.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1068 17592186554464 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1069 "q-340513" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1071 "Let $F(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ for $x\\in R.$ Suppose $f(x)$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F(x)$ from $x\\in [0,2\\pi )$ to $x\\in R.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nII. $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R$\n\nIII. $f(x+\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1072 #uuid "5964ffcf-bc61-5cdb-8ce3-fe3d629e8c05" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554574 1074 "Since $f$ is the $2\\pi $-periodic extension of $F,$ we have \n\n- $f(x+2\\pi )=f(x)$ for all $x\\in R,$ and\n\n- $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in [0,2\\pi ).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. This is exactly the definition of $2\\pi $-periodicity:\n\n$$\nf(x+2\\pi )=f(x)\n$$\n\nfor all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Notice that $F$ itself is $2\\pi $-periodic, because for any $x\\in R,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x+2\\pi )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)=F(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $F$ already satisfies the periodicity rule, the $2\\pi $-periodic extension agrees with $F$ everywhere, so $f(x)=F(x)$ for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- Statement III is false. For example, take $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+\\pi )=f(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})=-1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhile\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=F(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1068 17592186554534 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1069 "q-250943" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1071 "Find the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose tenth term is $-8$ and whose twelfth term is $-72.$ You're given that the common ratio is negative.\n\n$r=${{field-1}}" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1072 #uuid "5abf554f-7e7b-567c-9a3f-c62e189188a5" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554575 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{12}=-72$ and ${a}_{10}=-8.$ Substituting $n=12$ and $m=10$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{12-10} & =\\frac{{a}_{12}}{{a}_{10}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{(-72)}{(-8)} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is negative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =(-3)^{2} \\\\ r & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1068 17592186554487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1069 "q-138860" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1071 "Consider the function $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}.$ The derivative of this function is given by\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{g(x)}.\n$$\n\nFind the function $g(x).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1072 #uuid "5c789989-28b9-52e0-ac72-a0f8b7d66a60" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554576 1074 "Our function can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt[3]{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}}{\\sqrt[3]{3x+6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6/3}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, we take the natural logarithm of both sides and use the quotient and power rules for logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{2}}{(3x+6)^{1/3}}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(({x}^{2}+2)^{2})-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((3x+6)^{1/3}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we differentiate both sides with respect to $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+2)-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x+6)) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y}\\cdot {y}^{′} & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+2}\\cdot 2x-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3x+6}\\cdot 3 \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{′}}{y} & =\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we solve for ${y}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =y(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we substitute $y=\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x}{{x}^{2}+2}-\\frac{1}{3x+6}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{4x(3x+6)-({x}^{2}+2)}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\sqrt[3]{\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)^{6}}{3x+6}}(\\frac{11{x}^{2}+24x-2}{({x}^{2}+2)(3x+6)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{({x}^{2}+2)(11{x}^{2}+24x-2)}{\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\ng(x)=\\sqrt[3]{(3x-6)^{4}}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1068 17592186554503 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1069 "q-53189" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1071 "![](images/ff/ff93274daa7f179f86343e59b7cf277c0e70555fdfdd3cce113c3cba149b08ac.png)\n\nAn airplane $A$ is flying horizontally at a constant height of $800\\,\\text{m}$ above the ground. The angle of elevation $\\theta $ made by the airplane and a nearby radar station $R$ is decreasing at a rate of $0.05$ radians per second. What is the speed of the aircraft as a function of $\\theta ?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1072 #uuid "5cbfe710-aab6-5e49-b65f-e20759621640" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554577 1074 "![](images/29/29d06d071e6983d3487426b08d5a5548a611134dcf116674d199f6d3de03f246.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the airplane's altitude, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the radar station and the airplane. The quantity $y=800$ meters does not change with time because the airplane stays at a constant altitude.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{800}{x} \\\\ x & =800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(800\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.05$ (negative because the angle is decreasing). Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-800{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )(-0.05) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the aircraft is $40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\,\\text{m/s}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1068 17592186554514 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1069 "q-112010" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1072 #uuid "60969677-514c-5daf-874b-85d15a063d5f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554578 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a negative cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be negative:\n\n$$\n(x+4)(x+2)(2-x)=x\\cdot x\\cdot (-x)+⋯=-{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is given in the factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are  $x=-4,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=2.$ \n\nWe now find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0+4)(0+2)(2-0) \\\\  & =(4)(2)(2) \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,-2,2$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=16,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/06/06e69e393a48794262d45233bdd0df8f69f2b9ab8a3e9acee5aed3280db9f503.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1068 17592186554442 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1069 "q-134736" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1071 "A veterinarian collects data on a puppy's height $y$ (in inches) at certain times $x$ (in months) since it was born. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=2.2x+3.5.\n$$\n\nWhat is the residual after $5$ months if the actual height of the $5$-month old puppy is $15.5\\,in?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1072 #uuid "66a5e8a2-f155-513a-b41f-dffb1f1fb098" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554579 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the estimated height using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =2.2x+3.5 \\\\  & =2.2(5)+3.5 \\\\  & =14.5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =15.5-14.5 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $1\\,in.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1068 17592186554528 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1069 "q-97940" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1071 "![](images/e5/e5138b92d20572b1680dfc010d6e0f8729de9c21c62448cdcb472efbf762c4d1.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(-2x)?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1072 #uuid "689e7bfc-91b5-5578-9523-c9029e296a11" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554580 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nFirst, let's factor out the coefficient next to the $x$ inside the function:\n\n$$\ny=f(-(2x))\n$$\n\nTo plot $y=-f(-(2x)),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{2}$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(2x).$\n\n![](images/7d/7d76ce80b4212d0cb6cd8b64c424492dd46cad3819ce3cba17bab22c4df115f5.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(-2x).$\n\n![](images/de/ded37839584d99725ab44ebfb4e60e05ea162da6a3845817aa96f6904d67146a.png)\n\n- Finally, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(-2x).$\n\n![](images/6f/6f16d9eed1ed1b5fa41c46124f775238ecb759d79261cc66073c1fb304deb468.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1068 17592186554467 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1069 "q-159557" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1071 "| **Height ($x$)** | **$67$** | **$70$** | **$73$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Shoe size ($y$)** | $8.5$ | $9$ | $11$ | $13$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and corresponding shoe sizes of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=71.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.338,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.  \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1072 #uuid "69c89ef7-3131-5d48-9709-9716ca24bd0d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554581 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-26.6+0.5x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-26.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{0.338\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{71.5}^{2}}{45})}≈18.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-26.6\\pm 18.1\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1068 17592186554474 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1069 "q-86452" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)={e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,-1,{e}^{2}).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1072 #uuid "6a99613e-1f33-5aa3-a707-361661349677" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554582 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,-1,{e}^{2}):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{2x}-{y}^{2}z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2x}\\text{ }i-2yz\\text{ }j-{y}^{2}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,-1,{e}^{2}) & =2{e}^{2}\\text{ }i-2(-1)({e}^{2})\\text{ }j-(-1)^{2}k \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{2}\\,j-k \\\\  & =⟨2{e}^{2},2{e}^{2},-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,-1,{e}^{2})\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-{e}^{2}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2{e}^{2},2{e}^{2},-1⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y+1,\\,z-{e}^{2}⟩ & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1068 17592186554458 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1069 "q-123333" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=2{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-4,\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1072 #uuid "6b244d67-f2e6-5997-9db2-2abb0586d4be" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554583 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=2.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=2>1$, the sequence diverges." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1068 17592186554437 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1069 "q-86454" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1071 "Given the function $f(x,y,z)=2y{e}^{x}-{e}^{z}$, find the equation of the tangent plane to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(1,1,-1).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1072 #uuid "6cd59b40-dc86-5174-9cba-78e6f66091cf" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554584 1074 "The equation of the tangent plane to the level surface $f(x,y,z)=k$ at the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ can be expressed as\n\n$$\n∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩=0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})=(1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y{e}^{x}-{e}^{z})\\text{ }i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y{e}^{x}-{e}^{z})\\text{ }j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y{e}^{x}-{e}^{z})\\text{ }k \\\\  & =2y{e}^{x}\\text{ }i+2{e}^{x}\\text{ }j-{e}^{z}k \\\\ ∇\\,f(1,1,-1) & =2(1)e\\text{ }i+2e\\text{ }j-{e}^{-1}k \\\\  & =2e\\,i+2e\\,j-{e}^{-1}k \\\\  & =⟨2e,2e,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write down the Cartesian equation of the tangent plane to the level surface, as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})\\cdot ⟨x-{x}_{0},\\,y-{y}_{0},\\,z-{z}_{0}⟩ & =0 \\\\ ∇f(1,1,-1)\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z+1⟩ & =0 \\\\ ⟨2e,2e,-\\frac{1}{e}⟩\\cdot ⟨x-1,\\,y-1,\\,z+1⟩ & =0 \\\\ 2ex+2ey-\\frac{z}{e}-4e-\\frac{1}{e} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1068 17592186554431 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1069 "q-343183" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1071 "![](images/c4/c410aeaa3ceeafe3b072233a51020594370431e35244ca240637c5b124807983.png)\n\nA firefighter $F$ is lowering a rescue basket $B$ from a helicopter. The basket stays $40\\,\\text{ft}$ above the ground while it moves horizontally toward the firefighter at a speed of $12\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ Find, in radians per second as a function of $\\theta ,$ the rate at which the angle $\\theta $ between the cable and the horizontal is increasing.\n\nThe angle is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1072 #uuid "70ef2d3a-cac0-51c8-b6cc-9ac0c81e8893" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554585 1074 "![](images/cf/cfac2040d258398133397c5322e650bf60f59c7c372c97bfecec654eb3a4a278.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the basket's height, and $x$ is the horizontal distance between the firefighter and the basket. The quantity $y=40$ feet does not change with time because the basket height remains the same.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{40}{x} \\\\ x & =40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(40\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-12\\,\\text{ft/s}$ (negative because the horizontal distance is decreasing). Substituting this into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-12 & =(-40{csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{12}{40} & =({csc}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{40}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the angle is increasing at a rate of $\\frac{3}{10}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{rad/s}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1068 17592186554498 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1069 "q-29342" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}}$ given that $a\\ge 0.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1072 #uuid "758c8bb2-c32e-5b6a-b65e-f02ce4ad0d8b" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554586 1074 "We first write $32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}$ as the product $16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}\\cdot 2a$ which contains a perfect square $(16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}).$ Then, we separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{32{a}^{7}{b}^{8}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{16{a}^{6}{b}^{8}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4{a}^{3}{b}^{4})^{2}\\cdot 2a} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(4{a}^{3}{b}^{4})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ |4{a}^{3}{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ |4|\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA number raised to the power of four is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have that $|{b}^{4}|={b}^{4}$ and $|{a}^{3}|={a}^{3}.$ Therefore, our final answer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot |{a}^{3}|\\cdot |{b}^{4}|\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4\\cdot {a}^{3}\\cdot {b}^{4}\\cdot \\sqrt{2a} & = \\\\ 4{a}^{3}{b}^{4}\\sqrt{2a}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1068 17592186554508 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1069 "q-159454" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1071 "| **Age ($x$)** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** | **$7$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Price ($y$)** | $10.2$ | $9$ | $8.1$ | $5$ |\n\nThe table above shows the ages, in years, and corresponding selling prices, in thousands of dollars, of four car models as advertised in a newspaper classifieds last month. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.5,$ and $\\text{MSE}=0.456,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.92)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1072 #uuid "764e52d5-673e-5136-a2ff-4fbb20c5aad9" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554587 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=12.7-x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=12.7-x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-1.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(2-4.5)^{2}+(4-4.5)^{2}+(5-4.5)^{2}+(7-4.5)^{2} \\\\  & =6.25+0.25+0.25+6.25 \\\\  & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.456}{13}}≈0.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-1\\pm 0.5\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1068 17592186554445 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1069 "q-64032" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1071 "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose second term is $48$ and whose fifth term is $6.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1072 #uuid "7794f339-0fd4-50d9-a0ab-1ce4d82318cb" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554588 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=6$ and ${a}_{2}=48.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{6}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{8} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1068 17592186554522 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1069 "q-162234" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1071 "By changing the order of integration in the following repeated integrals so that they are integrated first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y,$ express the following sum as a single repeated integral.\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2z}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z+{\\int }_{1}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{3-z}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1072 #uuid "7da71e9d-63e8-5f19-957f-ca4c328a0eb6" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554589 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integrals, we see that the domain of integration $R={R}_{1}\\cup {R}_{2},$ where ${R}_{1}$ and ${R}_{2}$ are type II regions, is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{R}_{1} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 2z,\\,\\,-y\\le x\\le z\\}, \\\\ {R}_{2} & =\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-z,\\,\\,-y\\le x\\le z\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9b8806738b61c4ce692f555261abcd9918096a7585291954a7f11c39aadeab39.png)\n\nThe region $D$ consists of two type II regions in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 1,\\,0\\le y\\le 2z\\}\\,\\cup \\,\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 3,\\,0\\le y\\le 3-z\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is\n\n$$\ny=2z\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{y}{2}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\ny=3-z\\,⟹\\,z=3-y.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 2,\\,\\frac{y}{2}\\le z\\le 3-y\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/d1/d1a9d83672ffbd372b6a994c62771291d957e6a5c13189a6baea6d544f6a1bbc.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{y/2}^{3-y}{\\int }_{-y}^{z}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1068 17592186554515 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1069 "q-346663" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1071 "Let $D=\\{0,5,10\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{D}(7)?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1072 #uuid "7ecead24-f472-509d-9b09-182eae6554e7" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554590 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{D}(x)$ of a set $D$ equals $1$ if $x\\in D$ and $0$ if $x\\notin D.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(x)=1[x\\in D].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{D}(7)=1[7\\in D]=1[7\\in \\{0,5,10\\}].\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1068 17592186554504 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1069 "q-159561" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{12},{y}_{12})$ of $n=12$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=1.2,$ the mean square error of the model is $0.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=18.9,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.228)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $10$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1072 #uuid "812d00c8-ff0e-5dde-8e15-88d6aa199ddf" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554591 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.4+0.2x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.4.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{0.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{12}+\\frac{{1.2}^{2}}{18.9})}≈0.7\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.4\\pm 0.7\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1068 17592186554493 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1069 "q-42682" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1071 "Let $⟨x,y,z⟩$ be the unique solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}kx+2y-4z=-4 \\\\ x-2y+5z=-6 \\\\ 10x+y+2z=8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that  $x=-\\frac{20}{3}$ and that the determinant of the coefficient matrix is equal to $12-9k,$ find the value of $k.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1072 #uuid "8259ae37-435b-52c3-a2f0-cf085e62a133" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554592 1074 "First, let's write the determinant of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=|\\begin{aligned}k & 2 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 10 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|=12-9k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x}=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & 2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & -2 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & =(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 5 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & 5 \\\\ 8 & 2\\end{aligned}|+(-4)\\cdot |\\begin{aligned}-6 & -2 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-4(-4-5)-2(-12-40)-4(-6+16) \\\\  & =36+104-40 \\\\  & =100.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nx=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D}=\\frac{100}{12-9k}.\n$$\n\nFrom the problem statement, we know that $x=-\\frac{20}{3}.$ So, we can solve for $k$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{100}{12-9k} & =-\\frac{20}{3} \\\\ 15 & =-12+9k \\\\ 9k & =27 \\\\ k & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1068 17592186554510 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1069 "q-121713" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1071 "![](images/ec/ecb02db9984510219c6209392c9f35df52eca11dacc802a133aa51c4e5065ede.png)\n\nA drone $D$ takes off from a point $P$ and climbs with an angle of elevation of ${60}^{∘}$ and a vertical speed of $4\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Assuming the angle of elevation doesn't change, calculate the actual speed of the drone in the direction it is traveling.\nRound your answer to two decimal places." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1072 #uuid "847493a1-e66a-5ae4-9cf4-e1e68a84c19e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554593 1074 "![](images/69/69314419f6e0b93236fc048c6b9b6b262b09ff2cc061996353fbe23870a12726.png)\n\nLet $y$ denote the vertical distance from the drone's projection to the starting point, and $z$ be the distance between the drone and the starting point, as shown above.\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $y$ and $z.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ={60}^{∘},$ the sides $y$ and $z$ are the opposite and hypotenuse, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} & =\\frac{y}{z} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(z) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know that $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=4\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$ Substituting this into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}z}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\cdot 4≈4.62\n$$\n\nrounded to two decimal places.\n\nTherefore, the speed of the drone is approximately $4.62\\,\\text{km}/\\text{h}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1068 17592186554513 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1069 "q-159560" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=4.3,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.7,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=330,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1072 #uuid "851fa16f-b92a-5672-9bbf-979f258829aa" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554594 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=5+0.4x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=5+0.4x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{4.3}^{2}}{330})}≈2.5\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=5\\pm 2.5\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1068 17592186554492 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1069 "q-134788" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1071 "![](images/f9/f940a05c937b3158ce737959dee4685d216e6d55d6651672aa910cdb2c492f1e.png)\n\nThe scatter plot for the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1072 #uuid "855a07b7-e3c5-5f4d-b62e-d65fbbb7b6dd" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554595 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(5,1).$ So, our actual value is $y=1.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=5$ has the $y$-coordinate $0.$ So, our estimated value of is $\\overset{^}{y}=0.$\n\n![](images/93/937c0307a74881c87e6a33c47b32a1ac2d5124326b13538455640cd9fc88c14e.png)\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1068 17592186554479 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1069 "q-123334" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1071 "Consider the following geometric sequence:\n\n$$\n{a}_{n+1}=4{a}_{n},\\,{a}_{1}=-\\frac{1}{4},\\,n\\ge 1\n$$\n\nDetermine whether the sequence is convergent or divergent. If it converges, find the limit." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1072 #uuid "8875866b-8dd0-5b03-ad00-243a32baa5f0" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554596 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence is a geometric sequence with the common ratio \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}=4.\n$$\n\nSince $|r|=4>1$, the sequence diverges." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1068 17592186554455 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1069 "q-72561" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(2{s}^{-1}{t}^{2})}^{4}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1072 #uuid "88e971de-92ab-5f11-8659-2661a18f45d8" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554597 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2{s}^{-1}{t}^{2})}^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}\\cdot {({s}^{-1})}^{4}\\cdot {({t}^{2})}^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}\\cdot {s}^{-1\\cdot 4}\\cdot {t}^{2\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ 16{s}^{-4}{t}^{8} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1068 17592186554465 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1069 "q-43109" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1071 "Consider the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x-8y+3z=2 \\\\ 6y-3z=-7 \\\\ x-5y+4z=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, $y=\\frac{{D}_{y}}{D}$, where $D$ is the determinant of the coefficient matrix and ${D}_{y}=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1072 #uuid "8be0f924-0f3e-5631-9e67-77a4667752f3" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554598 1074 "We are given the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}5x+(-8)y+3z=2 \\\\ 0x+6y+(-3)z=-7 \\\\ 1x+(-5)y+4z=-10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\nD=|\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 6 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -5 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\n$$\n\nNow, to construct ${D}_{y}$, we replace the second column of the coefficient matrix by the right-hand side column of the given system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{y}=|\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 & 3 \\\\ 0 & -7 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -10 & 4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1068 17592186554536 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1069 "q-159590" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1071 "| **Height ($x$)** | **$64$** | **$68$** | **$72$** | **$76$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Weight ($y$)** | $130$ | $148$ | $164$ | $186$ |\n\nThe table above shows the heights, in inches, and the weights, in pounds, of four randomly selected men. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x.$\nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=70,$ and $\\text{MSE}=3.6,$ find a $90%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>2.920)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1072 #uuid "8c255cc5-a4ec-5d1f-be58-dbb45c0a4d57" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554599 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=-165+4.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-165+4.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=-165.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.920)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.920.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(64-70)^{2}+(68-70)^{2}+(72-70)^{2}+(76-70)^{2} \\\\  & =36+4+4+36 \\\\  & =80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.920\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{70}^{2}}{80})}≈43.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=-165\\pm 43.4\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1068 17592186554454 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1069 "q-112005" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1071 "Given that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x)={x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-9x+18,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1072 #uuid "8d4fa4d9-0aaf-5089-9a38-77f84d423e87" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554600 1074 "First, we note that the function is a positive cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is positive.\n\nWe are told that $(x-2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x-2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ 2 & 1 & -2 & -9 & 18 \\\\  &  & 2 & 0 & -18 \\\\  & 1 & 0 & -9 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x-2)({x}^{2}-9) \\\\  & =(x-2)(x-3)(x+3),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nso the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-3,$ $x=2,$ and $x=3.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(0-2)(0-3)(0+3) \\\\  & =(-2)(-3)(3) \\\\  & =18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-3,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=18,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/06/066d0669762137e2e014c2b76c941bdf7812c2d537ae1c03e0ae89a1d7107dc3.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1068 17592186554523 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1069 "q-141404" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1071 "Does the sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ for $n\\ge 1$ \nconverge or diverge? If it converges, what is its limit?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1072 #uuid "8f0ee2b9-8191-5f49-b4c7-7195eb067ecb" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554601 1074 "Suppose we have a geometric sequence with the first term ${a}_{1}$ and common ratio $r.$ Then, we have the following convergence properties:\n\n- if $|r|<1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to $0$\n\n- if $|r|>1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nMoreover, we have the following edge cases:\n\n- if $r=1,$ the sequence is convergent and converges to ${a}_{1}$\n\n- if $r=-1,$ the sequence is divergent\n\nThe given sequence ${a}_{n}=4{(\\frac{1}{2})}^{n-1}$ is a geometric sequence with the common ratio $r=\\frac{1}{2}.$ \n\nSince $|r|<1,$ the sequence converges to $0.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1068 17592186554511 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1069 "q-139035" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1070 17592186045486 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1071 "Consider the curve defined parametrically as \n\n$$\nx=2{e}^{t/2},\\,y={e}^{t},\\,t\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡3].\n$$\n\nCalculate the surface area generated when this curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1072 #uuid "8fe28743-a486-54a9-8fdd-bcbf858bcbbf" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554602 1074 "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $y$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi x\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince our curve is given in parametric form, we have\n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${t}_{1}=0$ to ${t}_{2}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.$ \n\nComputing the derivatives, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t/2})={e}^{t/2} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}x\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{({e}^{t/2})}^{2}+{({e}^{t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+{e}^{2t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}2{e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{{e}^{t}(1+{e}^{t})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t/2}\\cdot {e}^{t/2}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate the last integral using the substitution $u=1+{e}^{t}.$ Then, $\\text{d}u={e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We also need to compute new limits, given below.\n\n| **$t$** | $0$ | $\\operatorname{ln}⁡3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $u$ | $2$ | $4$ |\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}{e}^{t}\\sqrt{1+{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{2}^{4}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =4\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{2{u}^{3/2}}{3}|}_{2}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}({4}^{3/2}-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}(8-2\\sqrt{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{16\\pi }{3}(4-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1068 17592186554501 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1069 "q-72552" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${(2{a}^{-3})}^{4}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1072 #uuid "90c0de8b-ab19-5e68-9b86-0c6dd26a2646" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554603 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(2{a}^{-3})}^{4} & = \\\\ {2}^{4}{({a}^{-3})}^{4} & = \\\\ 16\\cdot {a}^{-3\\cdot 4} & = \\\\ 16{a}^{-12} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1068 17592186554490 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1069 "q-28058" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)$ where $f(x)=\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1072 #uuid "90f18038-3a01-51b3-920b-b432cec605da" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554604 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{6}(x-2)(x-3)(x+4)=\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=-4.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{6}(0-2)(0-3)(0+4) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-2)(-3)(4)}{6} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-4,2,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=4,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/1f/1f574e24288b0094defccb791407c74d9c3a5eeb1821b3bcac3bfa88153e69f5.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1068 17592186554516 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1069 "q-159541" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1071 "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $95%$ confidence interval for the slope of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>4.303)=0.025,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the slope is given by* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1072 #uuid "939b2026-f5f3-5f3b-a15d-5ba46809dfda" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554605 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.7.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>4.303)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.025}=4.303.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=4.303\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{7.7}{40}}≈1.9\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.7\\pm 1.9\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1068 17592186554520 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1069 "q-28059" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)(x-3)(x+5)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1072 #uuid "93eea4d8-b061-5509-a1b0-2eaeace6d46a" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554606 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{3}(x+1)(x-3)(x+5)=\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-1$, $x=3$ and $x=-5.$\n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{3}(0+1)(0-3)(0+5) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(-3)(5)}{3} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-5,-1,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-5,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/05/0514909ce208ebf90584d7130705b5eccc0db08596d558dd83467b7cfda925fa.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554607 1068 17592186554480 