t{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.4+0.2x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.2.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $95%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.95=0.05\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.025.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.228)=0.025.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{10,0.025}=2.228.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.228\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.7}{18.9}}≈0.4\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.2\\pm 0.4\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1068 17592186554527 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1069 "q-120787" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1071 "In the regular octagon $ABCDEFGH$, we have $CD=1.5\\,cm.$ What is the perimeter of the octagon?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1072 #uuid "9bda3ed0-c16c-5140-97eb-7ad72121a660" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554609 1074 "The perimeter $p$ of a polygon is the sum of the measures of all its sides. Since all the sides of a regular octagon are equal and the measure of one of them is $1.5\\,cm$, we have \n\n$$\np=8\\cdot CD=8\\cdot 1.5=12\\,cm.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1068 17592186554477 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1069 "q-134719" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=-1.5x+2.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=12$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $-14?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1072 #uuid "9d55db4e-8bf0-577f-a32c-7bdda150fe93" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554610 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =-1.5x+2 \\\\  & =-1.5(12)+2 \\\\  & =-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =-14-(-16) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1068 17592186554450 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1069 "q-97783" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1071 "The graph of $y=f(-(x+5))$ can be obtained from the graph of $y=f(x)$ by applying **some** of the following transformations:\n\n- $(\\text{T}1)$ - Reflection across the $x$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}2)$ - Translation by $5$ units to the left\n\n- $(\\text{T}3)$ - Stretch of scale factor $5$ parallel to the $y$-axis\n\n- $(\\text{T}4)$ - Reflection across the $y$-axis\n\nWhich of the following could be a possible ordering of the appropriate transformations?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1072 #uuid "a0d0470d-b24d-5636-b2cb-b9c005b7e95d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554611 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo get $y=f(-(x+5)),$ we can apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}4):$ Reflect $y=f(x)$ across the $y$-axis to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-x).\n$$\n\n- Apply transformation $(\\text{T}2):$ Translate $y=f(-x)$ to the left by $5$ units to get the graph of \n\n$$\ny=f(-(x+5)).\n$$\n\nTherefore, a possible order of transformations is\n\n$\\,$ $(\\text{T}4)\\to (\\text{T}2).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1068 17592186554507 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1069 "q-64033" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1071 "Find the common ratio of the geometric sequence whose fourth term is $135$ and whose seventh term is $5.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1072 #uuid "a5c77383-51aa-56e3-a7b4-492c5c358798" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554612 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula \n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{7}=5$ and ${a}_{4}=135.$ Substituting $n=7$ and $m=4$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{7-4} & =\\frac{{a}_{7}}{{a}_{4}} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{5}{135} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by solving the equation for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1068 17592186554500 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1069 "q-159596" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1071 "| **Height ($x$)** | **$60$** | **$62$** | **$66$** | **$68$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **IQ  ($y$)** | $110$ | $106$ | $108$ | $102$ |\n\nThe table above gives the heights, in inches, and the corresponding scores on an IQ test of four high school students. The corresponding linear regression line fitted using this sample is $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x.$ \nAssuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=64,$ and $\\text{MSE}=7.7,$ find a $98%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>6.965)=0.01,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $2$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for the $y$-intecept is* \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1072 #uuid "a77fcdca-ce6d-5371-a7a2-e886bf8d2462" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554613 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=151.3-0.7x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\beta }_{0}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=151.3-0.7x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=151.3.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $98%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.98=0.02\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.01.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>6.965)=0.01.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.01}=6.965.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(60-64)^{2}+(62-64)^{2}+(66-64)^{2}+(68-64)^{2} \\\\  & =16+4+4+16 \\\\  & =40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=6.965\\cdot \\sqrt{7.7\\cdot (\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{{64}^{2}}{40})}≈195.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=151.3\\pm 195.8\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1068 17592186554440 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1069 "q-31605" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1071 "A horizontal line $l$ passes through the point $(5,4).$ Calculate the coordinates of the $y$-intercept of $l.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1072 #uuid "aa2d0b41-12c5-5cc7-a265-9b2befe09045" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554614 1074 "On any horizontal line, all points have the same $y$-coordinate.\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the point $(5,4)$ is $4,$ so the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept is also $4.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,4).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1068 17592186554461 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1069 "q-162495" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1071 "![](images/2f/2f95783c1e9624e58b4cd26764e94fb61d45c7aa75260ce8ca726a6c2e5db9ab.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{3-3z/2}^{3}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $y,$ then $z,$ and then $x.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1072 #uuid "ac81bb63-6896-5260-96a8-5a88cb11f40d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554615 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type III region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,8-\\frac{4x}{3}-2z\\le y\\le 4\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,z)}^{{u}_{2}(x,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $y,$ the $y$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(x)}^{{v}_{2}(x)}{\\int }_{8-4x/3-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $xz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/85/85d184f328ebdbed5faf2d1c43261940a8241b6637450ab5fe1c8d803026e273.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $xz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le 2,\\,\\,3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\le x\\le 3\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the bottom  boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(x),$ is \n\n$$\nx=3-\\frac{3z}{2}\\,⟹\\,z=2-\\frac{2x}{3}\n$$\n\nand the top boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=2.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 3,\\,\\,2-\\frac{2x}{3}\\le z\\le 2\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/6a/6ac58e4c98c79a1a698d052479b55a45dd2b79bed4ca11422d172fe306dac15f.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{2-2x/3}^{2}{\\int }_{8-4/3x-2z}^{4}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1068 17592186554482 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1069 "q-37159" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1071 "$$\nB=\\{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\},\\,[x]_{S}=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the coordinates of the vector $x$ relative to the standard basis $S$ are given above. Find $[x]_{B}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1072 #uuid "af173c80-5734-52f5-887a-4f2d2e37a60e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554616 1074 "We will use the change-of-coordinates formula in the form\n\n$$\n[\\,x\\,]_{B}={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S}.\n$$\n\nFirst, recall that the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is the matrix whose columns are the vectors of $B$ relative to the standard basis $S.$ As a result, we have\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we need to find the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{S\\to B}$ from $S$ to $B.$ To do that, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{S\\to B} & ={P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-(-2)\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we compute $[\\,x\\,]_{B}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\,x\\,]_{B} & ={P}_{S\\to B}[\\,x\\,]_{S} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{2} & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554617 1068 17592186554472 13194139535995 17592