itten as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=-26.6+0.5x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=0.5.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. \n\n- Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>2.92)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{2,0.05}=2.92.\n$$\n\n- Computing ${S}_{xx},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} & =(67-71.5)^{2}+(70-71.5)^{2}+(73-71.5)^{2}+(76-71.5)^{2} \\\\  & =20.25+2.25+2.25+20.25 \\\\  & =45.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Now, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=2.92\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{0.338}{45}}≈0.3\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=0.5\\pm 0.3\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1068 17592186554538 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1069 "q-159379" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{18},{y}_{18})$ of $n=18$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $2.53,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=484,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.746)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $16$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1072 #uuid "c51957e8-149e-57cd-8ee2-fb199eb311cd" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554620 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=1.5+3.8x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=1.5+3.8x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=3.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.746)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{16,0.05}=1.746.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.746\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{2.53}{484}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=3.8\\pm 0.1\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1068 17592186554428 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1069 "q-67513" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1071 "Find the equation for the common ratio $r$ of the geometric sequence whose $3$rd term is $4$ and whose $5$th term is $196.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1072 #uuid "c8e2a0b2-6f85-51c0-a653-f0669492c9a0" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554621 1074 "The formula for the $n$th term of a geometric sequence is given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}{r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula with $n=5$ and $n=3,$ respectively, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{3} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{3-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{2}, \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}{r}^{5-1}={a}_{1}{r}^{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nGiven that ${a}_{3}=4$ and ${a}_{5}=196,$ we obtain the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1}{r}^{2}=4 \\\\ {a}_{1}{r}^{4}=196\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, by dividing the second equation by the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{r}^{4}}{{a}_{1}{r}^{2}} & =\\frac{196}{4} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{4}}{\\cancel{\\,{a}_{1}\\,}{r}^{2}} & =\\frac{196}{4} \\\\ \\frac{{r}^{4}}{{r}^{2}} & =49 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =49.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1068 17592186554499 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1069 "q-314883" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1071 "![](images/7d/7de8ecd3d178cf07377aef5ca96ce28f638700e6284d151e3578ba11ed87327a.png)\n\nEthan notices a drone descending. He estimates that the distance between his location and the point on the ground directly beneath the drone is $150\\,\\text{ft}.$ If the angle of elevation $\\theta $ between him and the drone decreases at a rate of $0.1\\,\\text{rad/s},$ at what rate is its altitude decreasing when $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}?$ Assume that the rate of decreasing altitude is **positive** and do not round the answer.\n\nThe drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n$\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1072 #uuid "d00df18b-1c5e-50a7-89f5-e1bc96838901" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554622 1074 "![](images/50/5024a642ac27698d848577165b08e3158d3248d50c064a79432fcad6e2df90d5.png)\n\nIn the figure, $y$ is the drone’s altitude, and $x$ is the distance between Ethan and the shadow. The quantity $x=150\\,\\text{ft}$ does not change with time because the distance between Ethan and the shadow is fixed.\n\nWe wish to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.$ To do this, let's start by setting up a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Relative to the angle $\\theta ,$ the sides $x$ and $y$ are the adjacent and opposite, respectively. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{y}{150} \\\\ y & =150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate both sides of the equation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(150\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta ) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡\\theta \\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given information, we have $\\frac{\\text{d}\\theta }{\\text{d}t}=-0.1$ (negative because the angle is decreasing) and $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Substituting these values into the above, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150{sec}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{3})(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =150(4)(-0.1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =-60.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the drone's altitude is decreasing at a rate of $60\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1068 17592186554521 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1069 "q-102710" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1071 "Consider the surface ${e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}=e-3.$ Given that $n=⟨a,b,c⟩$ is a non-zero normal vector to the surface at the point $(1,1,-1),$ what is the value of $\\frac{a-b}{c}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1072 #uuid "d5913a4b-d974-52bc-82fc-74c4bb66f8ca" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554623 1074 "Let's define  $f(x,y,z)={e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}.$ Then, our surface can be viewed as the level surface $f(x,y,z)=e-3.$  \n\nNow, we find the gradient of $f(x,y,z)$ and evaluate it at $(1,1,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\,j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2}))j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({e}^{xy}-{x}^{2}-2{z}^{2})k \\\\  & =(y{e}^{xy}-2x)i+(x{e}^{xy})j+(-4z)k \\\\ ∇f(1,1,-1) & =((1){e}^{(1)(1)}-2(1))\\text{ }i+(1){e}^{(1)(1)}\\text{ }j+(-4)(-1)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =(e-2)\\text{ }i+e\\text{ }j+4\\text{ }k \\\\  & =⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our normal vector is $n=⟨a,b,c⟩=⟨e-2,\\,e,\\,4⟩.$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a-b}{c}=\\frac{(e-2)-e}{4}=-\\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1068 17592186554525 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1069 "q-115013" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1071 "![](images/85/8536196127d1e8fc92cd2d442005a386b2d981a027a497ecce363559f52ac0e6.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1072 #uuid "d8c07848-a1dd-5eb7-8320-0b4777fcd645" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554624 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4,$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{5}{2}$ parallel to the $y$-axis to get the graph of $y=\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](images/ba/bafacd18cae6114ad22fb59d905a8ebb68cc8768a5b20a4a5a2e36f59c11ad11.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x).$\n\n![](images/bd/bd69db18de3d58e6806b7ee3473a0fd4e8b6df384cf371be4e9648cac53e3927.png)\n\n- Finally, translate the result down by $4$ units to get the graph of $y=-\\frac{5}{2}f(x)-4.$\n\n![](images/fe/fe2e7245978e120a538cc87a31d6180c4e342fc438240be1f3ce367eed64bb33.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1068 17592186554451 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1069 "q-28492" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1071 "Which of the following curves is generated by $y=\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1072 #uuid "dae1615e-910b-570a-9c8d-abe635df0b2e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554625 1074 "First, notice that the function is a product of $3$ linear binomials, so this function is a cubic function. Moreover, it is a positive cubic because if we were to expand the parentheses, the coefficient of the ${x}^{3}$ term would be positive:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{5}(x+1)(x+2)(x-5)=\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot x\\cdot x\\cdot x+⋯=\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{3}+⋯\n$$\n\nThe equation is already in factored form. So by the zero-product rule, we deduce that the roots are $x=-1,$ $x=-2,$ and $x=5.$ \n\nWe also find the $y$-intercept of the curve by plugging the value $x=0$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =\\frac{1}{5}(0+1)(0+2)(0-5) \\\\  & =\\frac{(1)(2)(-5)}{5} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an inverted $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,5$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-2,$ as follows:\n\n \n\n![](images/e2/e27e18b8e79c71dcc888e62c4472723b44b1b4fa0367e124313f95bc08ef70fa.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1068 17592186554530 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1069 "q-347438" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=-6,\\,{x}_{2}=-1,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=7\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1072 #uuid "e3d61585-203e-5ae2-ba7d-32b154b3cecc" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554626 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1$ equals $2{x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $1$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1\n$$\n\nadds $2{x}_{i}$ for nonnegative values and $1$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-6\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[-1\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[4\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[7\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot (2{x}_{i})+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 1 & =1+1+2\\cdot 0+2\\cdot 4+2\\cdot 7 \\\\  & =1+1+0+8+14 \\\\  & =24.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1068 17592186554495 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1069 "q-36187" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1071 "If $A=\\{3,5,7,19,21,115\\}$ and $B=\\{2,3,4,7,16,19,21,22\\},$ then $A\\cup B=$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1072 #uuid "e3ebe755-cc28-56a9-a6f9-b3ea716cdfc2" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554627 1074 "The set $A\\cup B$ is the set of all elements belonging to either $A$ or $B,$ or both. So, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\cup B & =\\{3,5,7,19,21,115\\}\\cup \\{2,3,4,7,16,19,21,22\\} \\\\  & =\\{2,3,4,5,7,16,19,21,22,115\\}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1068 17592186554438 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1069 "q-27983" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1071 "Given that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+7x+6,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1072 #uuid "e4fd339f-d602-5df0-954c-b8f9a8d50336" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554628 1074 "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+1)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by $(x+1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 7 & 6 \\\\  &  & 1 & -1 & -6 \\\\  & -1 & 1 & 6 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+1)(-{x}^{2}+x+6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)({x}^{2}-x-6) \\\\  & =-(x+1)(x+2)(x-3).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-1,x=-2,$ and $x=3.$\n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+1)(0+2)(0-3) \\\\  & =-(1)(2)(-3) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an $S$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,-1,3$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=6,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/d8/d8bd6f9c8cbfd1872871a00d403e3e8980c081b03caac5f2c5cc656161a00cd5.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1068 17592186554470 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1069 "q-127310" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1071 "Use the flux form of Green's theorem to calculate the flux of the vector field\n\n$$\nF(x,y)=⟨{e}^{2y}+4x,\\,{x}^{7}-{y}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nthrough the boundary of the square $-1\\le x\\le 0,-1\\le y\\le 0.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1072 #uuid "e6203f86-9cf0-5174-84bf-8575e36ffa78" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554629 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j,$ and $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then the flux form of Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s={∬}_{D}\\text{div}\\,F\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09acd3df62b333bdaeb7553599507d4dbad7412ef2770b00e8fc8855b38a774e.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={e}^{2y}+4x$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{7}-{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂x}=4,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂y} & =-2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can express the rectangular region $D$ as follows:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(x,y):-1\\le x\\le 0,\\,-1\\le y\\le 0\\}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem in flux form, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}s & ={∮}_{C}P\\,dy-Q\\,dx \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂P}{∂x}+\\frac{∂Q}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}{\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)[x]_{-1}^{0}\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\cdot 1\\,dy \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{0}(4-2y)\\,dy \\\\  & =[4y-{y}^{2}]_{-1}^{0} \\\\  & =0-(4(-1)-(-1)^{2}) \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1068 17592186554469 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1068 17592186554485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1069 "q-171725" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1071 "A horizontal line $l$ passes through the point $(-3,2).$ What are the coordinates of the $y$-intercept of $l?$\n\nThe coordinates of the $y$-intercept of $l$ are\n$($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1072 #uuid "e629f775-4d70-5fe8-b2f0-8f18384b864c" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554630 1074 "On any horizontal line, all points have the same $y$-coordinate.\n\nThe $y$-coordinate of the point $(-3,2)$ is $2,$ so the $y$-coordinate of the $y$-intercept is also $2.$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the $y$-intercept are $(0,2).$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1068 17592186554481 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1069 "q-134753" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=1.8x+1.4.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=7$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $12?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1072 #uuid "e8526d11-e962-5974-8b9d-23623deb26c8" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554631 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =1.8x+1.4 \\\\  & =1.8(7)+1.4 \\\\  & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =12-14 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1068 17592186554488 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1069 "q-346662" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1071 "Let $C=\\{-3,-1,1,3\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{C}(-1)?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1072 #uuid "e8cf80bf-23f2-50e5-8be6-fd421cafc0d7" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554632 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{C}(x)$ of a set $C$ equals $1$ if $x\\in C$ and $0$ if $x\\notin C.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(x)=1[x\\in C].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{C}(-1)=1[-1\\in C]=1[-1\\in \\{-3,-1,1,3\\}].\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1068 17592186554476 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1069 "q-63999" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1071 "The second term of a geometric sequence is $3$ and its fourth term is $27.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1072 #uuid "e964bcbb-bd9b-5437-9046-a26f2773d2b6" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554633 1074 "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{4}=27$ and ${a}_{2}=3.$ Substituting $n=4$ and $m=2$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-2} & =\\frac{{a}_{4}}{{a}_{2}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{27}{3} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =9 \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={3}^{2} \\\\ r & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1068 17592186554439 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1069 "q-134830" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1071 "![](images/31/31a72edb688af9be0481fc98346aa5655190e32564ddc003eb7bdd1c83d41ee1.png)\n\nAn economist records data on the volume of corn exports $y$ (in thousands of tons) from the United States to a particular country, $x$ number of years since $2010.$\nThe residual plot above was constructed after fitting a linear regression model on this data set. Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. When $t=6,$ the regression equation underestimates the value of $y.$\n\nII. The residuals appear randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\nIII. This linear regression is probably a good fit for the data." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1072 #uuid "e996d8e3-bba2-534b-9d62-28bb3781b36f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554634 1074 "Let's examine the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. The residual equals the actual value minus the estimated value:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \nFrom the plot, the residual at $t=6$ is negative. So, we have that $\\text{Actual}<\\text{Estimated},$ and therefore the model overestimates (*not* underestimates) this data point.\n\n- Statement II is true. The plot shows that the residuals appear randomly scattered about the horizontal axis (there are no obvious trends).\n\n- Statement III is true. Since the residuals are close to $0$ and are randomly scattered above and below the horizontal axis, we can conclude that our regression line is probably a good fit for the given data.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1068 17592186554496 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1069 "q-67871" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1071 "In a quadrilateral $ABCD,$ the measures of the sides are $AB=17\\,mm,$ $BC=14\\,mm,$ $CD=2\\,cm,$ and $DA=9\\,mm.$\nWhat is the perimeter of the quadrilateral?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1072 #uuid "ef8d594b-7df8-58f6-be78-cc667ee70380" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554635 1074 "Most of the measures are given in $mm$ (millimeters), so we will start by converting all the measures to $mm$ and adding them up:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p & =17\\,mm+14\\,mm+\\overset{2\\,cm}{\\overset{⏞}{20\\,mm}}+9\\,mm \\\\  & =60\\,mm\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since the perimeter is a large quantity of $\\text{mm},$ we will convert to $\\text{cm}:$\n\n$$\np=60\\,mm=6\\,cm.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1068 17592186554429 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1069 "q-346677" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=9,\\,{x}_{2}=-8,\\,{x}_{3}=-1,\\,{x}_{4}=4,\\,{x}_{5}=2\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1072 #uuid "f030361e-8a29-5e63-bca8-4e28e1ba5da0" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554636 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[9>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[-8>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[4>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =1\\cdot 9+0\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 4+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =9+0+0+4+2 \\\\  & =15.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1068 17592186554529 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1069 "q-112024" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1071 "Given that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x)=-{x}^{3}+5{x}^{2}+2x-24,$ which of the following curves is generated by $y=f(x)?$\n\n(*Note: Figures not drawn to scale*)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1072 #uuid "f1e2e562-7654-5d22-adb4-85fe265b1c2b" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554637 1074 "First, we notice that the function is a negative cubic curve because the coefficient of ${x}^{3}$ is negative.\n\nWe are told that $(x+2)$ is a factor of $f(x),$ so to find the other roots of $f(x)$ we use synthetic division to divide $f(x)$ by  $(x+2):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {x}^{3} & {x}^{2} & {x}^{1} & {x}^{0} \\\\ -2 & -1 & 5 & 2 & -24 \\\\  &  & 2 & -14 & 24 \\\\  & -1 & 7 & -12 & 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(x+2)(-{x}^{2}+7x-12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)({x}^{2}-7x+12) \\\\  & =-(x+2)(x-3)(x-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting the factors equal to zero, we find that the roots of $f(x)$ are $x=-2,$ $x=3,$ and $x=4.$ \n\nNow, let's find the $y$-intercept. To do that, we substitute $x=0,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-(0+2)(0-3)(0-4) \\\\  & =-(2)(-3)(-4) \\\\  & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we draw an  $\\text{S}$-shaped graph that passes through the three roots $x=-2,3,4$ and has the $y$-intercept $y=-24,$ as follows:\n\n![](images/e4/e41620d89bb720602c0271428847e05a6655b8ff1e037c0b408972ceaf252cec.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1068 17592186554432 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1069 "q-159563" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{10},{y}_{10})$ of $n=10$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, $\\overset{―}{x}=2.7,$ the mean square error of the model is $3.6,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=82.5,$ find a $99%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept of the regression line.\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>3.355)=0.005,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $8$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1072 #uuid "f24d9e6f-29a8-5c79-a6cb-e6a041461fd9" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554638 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=2.8-3.3x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the $y$-intercept ${\\beta }_{0}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{{{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}}_{⏟}}_{\\,\\,\\text{point}\\,\\text{estimate}}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}$ is our point estimate for the intercept, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\text{MSE}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{n}+\\frac{{\\overset{―}{x}}^{2}}{{S}_{xx}})}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=2.8-3.3x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}=2.8.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $99%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.99=0.01\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.005.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>3.355)=0.005.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{8,0.005}=3.355.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=3.355\\cdot \\sqrt{3.6\\cdot (\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{{2.7}^{2}}{82.5})}≈2.8\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}\\,]=2.8\\pm 2.8\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1068 17592186554430 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1069 "q-159398" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1071 "Let $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x$ be the regression line fitted using a sample $({x}_{1},{y}_{1}),\\,…,\\,({x}_{9},{y}_{9})$ of $n=9$ paired observations. Assuming that all conditions for the simple regression model are satisfied, the mean square error of the model is $1.16,$ and ${S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}=240,$ find a $90%$  confidence interval for the slope of the regression line. \n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $P(T>1.895)=0.05,$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $7$ degrees of freedom. Also, the standard error for ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}.\n$$\n\n*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1072 #uuid "f28f1c0e-4ffd-5dee-98b7-1c475d5129ec" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554639 1074 "We use the following notations:\n\n- $y={\\beta }_{0}+{\\beta }_{1}x$ is the regression line that best fits the population, and\n\n- $\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x$ is the estimate of the \"true\" regression line given by the data in our sample.\n\nIn this case, we have that  \n\n$$\n\\overset{^}{\\,y}={\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{0}+{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}x=3.4-0.6x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that all assumptions for a simple regression model are held. So, given a value $\\alpha $ between $0$ and $1,$ the corresponding $[100(1-\\alpha )]%$ confidence interval for the slope ${\\beta }_{1}$ of our model can be written as\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,\\overset{\\text{margin of error}}{\\overset{⏞}{{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]}}.\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- $\\,{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}$ is our point estimate for the slope, computed using the data points from our sample.\n\n- $P(T>{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2})=\\frac{\\alpha }{2},$ where $T$ has a student's $t$-distribution with $n-2$ degrees of freedom.\n\n- $\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]$ is the standard error of ${\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1},$ given by\n\n$$\n\\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=\\sqrt{\\frac{\\text{MSE}}{{S}_{xx}}}\n$$\n\nwhere we have:\n\n- $\\text{MSE}$ is the mean squared error of the model:\n\n$$\n\\text{MSE}=\\frac{1}{n-2}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-{\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i})^{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\overset{^}{\\,y}}_{i}$ is the estimate of ${y}_{i}$ given by our regression model.\n\n- ${S}_{xx}$ is the usual sum of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{S}_{xx}={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}\n$$\n\nTo construct the confidence interval, we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1.** Find the point estimate. From the regression equation $\\overset{^}{\\,y}=3.4-0.6x,$ our point estimate is\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}=-0.6.\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Find the margin of error. Since we are interested in finding a $90%$ confidence interval, we have \n\n$$\n\\alpha =1-0.9=0.1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\alpha }{2}=0.05.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $P(T>1.895)=0.05.$ As a result, \n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}={t}_{7,0.05}=1.895.\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the margin of error:\n\n$$\n{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=1.895\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1.16}{240}}≈0.1\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** Determine the limits of the confidence interval:\n\n$$\n{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,\\pm \\,{t}_{n-2,\\alpha /2}\\cdot \\text{SE}[{\\overset{^}{\\,\\beta }}_{1}\\,]=-0.6\\pm 0.1\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1068 17592186554456 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1069 "q-162326" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1071 "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1072 #uuid "f35d7c5d-0e2d-5ee0-b1c5-16c26213b1d0" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554640 1074 "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/ed/ed7308cf6c77f5d89b94ec90237fa9161fc85429182813f38b55d916bdbdaeef.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/4f/4fb3323b2c295df8351910890f20266840b131a4dfd8a7bba0d61a3eb03bea23.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}[\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡z)}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[(1+0)^{3/2}-(1+1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1068 17592186554468 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1069 "q-33483" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1071 "Simplify $\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}}.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1072 #uuid "f380d0a4-2ab4-5ffd-9f65-88568a9b06d4" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554641 1074 "We first write $20{n}^{5}$ as the product $4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n$ which contains a perfect square $(4{n}^{4}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2{n}^{2}\\sqrt{5n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1068 17592186554448 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1069 "q-134737" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1071 "Consider the data sets $x$ and $y$ of paired observations. The equation of the corresponding linear regression line for this data is \n\n$$\ny=0.75x+2.25.\n$$\n\nAccording to the model, what is the residual at $x=8$ if the actual value of $y$ corresponding to this value of $x$ is $11.25?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1072 #uuid "f64a4538-a38b-5427-b753-a3e4f5424a55" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554642 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we compute the predicted value using our linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{y} & =0.75x+2.25 \\\\  & =0.75(8)+2.25 \\\\  & =8.25\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =11.25-8.25 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1068 17592186554441 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1069 "q-114069" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1071 "![](images/93/938babc27340209a1a5477fb3184abbf155f409c520c76995c4e234f99691347.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the function $y=f(x).$ Which of the following gives the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3})?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1072 #uuid "f73ff2c0-b98a-5833-8e43-d855656798f9" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554643 1074 "To perform transformations of functions with multiple operations, we must remember the following rule:\n\n1\\. First, we perform the transformations *inside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out horizontal stretches and reflections, followed by horizontal translations.\n\n2\\. Then, we perform the transformations *outside* the argument of $f.$ We first carry out vertical stretches and reflections, followed by vertical translations.\n\nTo plot $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}),$ we apply the following steps:\n\n- Take the curve $y=f(x).$\n\n- Stretch it by a scale factor of $\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{3})}=3$ parallel to the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/f0/f0b289d42620a275eeb90d4bdbe265ca92ea4264de70ecc11e5f661a84149628.png)\n\n- Then, reflect the result across the $x$-axis to get the graph of $y=-f(\\frac{x}{3}).$\n\n![](images/59/597ba19a0c993ea597ecd091df02d97e974364125c79780df04aef365442abb6.png)" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1068 17592186554443 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1069 "q-72556" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1071 "Which of the following is equivalent to ${({x}^{-2}y)}^{-3}?$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1072 #uuid "fc3c433e-4980-575f-8e3b-31e27fc92890" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554644 1074 "We apply the power of product rule and then simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({x}^{-2}y)}^{-3} & = \\\\ {({x}^{-2})}^{-3}{y}^{-3} & = \\\\ {x}^{-2(-3)}\\cdot {y}^{-3} & = \\\\ {x}^{6}{y}^{-3} & \\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1068 17592186554447 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1069 "q-134797" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1070 17592186045487 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1071 "![](images/81/815ba0a13bc8cbd7adcfadc63b80d6f2da36c9755166694915fa2f297be27721.png)\n\nAn employee of a car dealership collects data on the value $y$ (in thousands of dollars) of a particular car model $x$ years after its release to the market. The scatter plot for the data and the corresponding linear regression line are shown above.\n\nAccording to the regression model, what is the residual for the highlighted datapoint?" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1072 #uuid "fd84baa2-dbd3-5523-aca7-04ab311e0400" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554645 1074 "The residual equals the difference between the actual value of the dependent variable and the value estimated using the linear regression model:\n\n$$\n\\text{Residual}=\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated}\n$$\n\n \n\nThe highlighted datapoint has coordinates $(6,12).$ So, our actual value is $y=12.$\n\nOn the other hand, the point on the regression line that corresponds to $x=6$ has the $y$-coordinate $10.$ So, our estimated value is $\\overset{^}{y}=10.$\n\n![](images/ab/ab5e194ac03482b1cee6bdb7aa35e323b40a1ad63769e60acbb000adb7aa1e82.png)\n\nAs a result, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Residual} & =\\text{Actual}-\\text{Estimated} \\\\  & =y-\\overset{^}{y} \\\\  & =12-10 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the residual is $$2\\,000.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1068 17592186554517 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1069 "q-162329" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1070 17592186045485 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1071 "![](images/8f/8fc551f1439ed5a551ae8fd18234479bb062d43ae9e4faf55d896739f76e0f4d.png)\n\nThe triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over the region $R$ (shown above) can be expressed as the following repeated integral:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/z}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\nRewrite this repeated integral by integrating first with respect to $x,$ then $z,$ and then $y.$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1072 #uuid "fff4e417-ea88-59e6-bd63-239378f4d2c6" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554646 1074 "From the given repeated integral, we see that $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le 4-yz\\}\n$$\n\nWe wish to rewrite our triple integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nSince both repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(y)}^{{v}_{2}(y)}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](images/9a/9a59198b60398e2b3044296c3cc76b4748785303029dccc1087320cd11cb7d55.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is a type II region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le z\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le y\\le \\frac{4}{z}\\}\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y,$ we write $D$ as a type I region.\n\nNotice that the top boundary of the region, written in the form $z=z(y),$ is \n\n$$\ny=\\frac{4}{z}\\,⟹\\,z=\\frac{4}{y}\n$$\n\nand the bottom boundary of the region is\n\n$$\nz=1.\n$$\n\nThen, the type I representation of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,\\,1\\le z\\le \\frac{4}{y}\\},\n$$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](images/9b/9bc88aa4f0ad07d89ff5dd3c0de16208cb8bf47d774b557a7bf2140790c95291.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{1}^{4/y}{\\int }_{0}^{4-yz}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.\n$$" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554647 1062 #uuid "0c2f224c-fc34-5e4b-af33-9729624ea81e" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554647 1063 17592186554567 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554648 1062 #uuid "0c9bd898-fab7-54c1-83ae-93d8a8161ea8" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554648 1063 17592186554609 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554649 1058 17592186554663 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554649 1062 #uuid "0e4446b3-5739-5e1b-a31f-10c169910b46" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554649 1063 17592186554626 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554650 1062 #uuid "0e56b147-81e4-5b76-ba78-f42888fc798d" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554650 1063 17592186434757 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554651 1058 17592186554747 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554651 1062 #uuid "0eab5434-b5a9-5a0f-ba85-3d586bfabf06" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554651 1063 17592186326896 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554652 1058 17592186554746 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554652 1062 #uuid "1a33946e-506b-53c6-b93a-9bf44559027f" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554652 1063 17592186399329 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554653 1058 17592186554693 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554653 1062 #uuid "1aa63779-111f-54e9-892c-0fb614322783" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554653 1063 17592186554602 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554654 1058 17592186554689 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554654 1062 #uuid "1cc91ec0-2b1b-58e6-ae97-b3acdb0e54b9" 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554654 1063 17592186129751 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554655 1058 17592186554692 13194139535995 17592186045417 true] [17592186554655 1062 #uuid "1d2cdc91-a374-5d20-8c30-4a54336a8b0d" 13194139535995 1759218604541